al app versus just making the Xcode preview look polished. I think the cleanest approach is to show sample alarm cards when the list is empty, maybe with a \"範例\" label to distinguish them from real alarms. The preview already has sample data in the `#Preview` block, so the main change needed is wrapping AlarmCard in WatercolorCard and then handling the empty state in the live app by displaying a few example alarms.\n\nI'll add a static `sampleAlarms` property to show when the list is empty — this covers both the Xcode Preview looking good and the runtime empty state not feeling bare.\n\nAdding NavigationStack to ContentView...\n\nNow I'm laying out the implementation plan: add the subtitle font helper, create the DaytimeScene enum, update HomeView with proper fonts and the avatar, refactor AlarmListView to use WatercolorCard with sample data fallback, wrap ContentView in NavigationStack, and optionally refactor TotoroAvatar's timer logic.\n\nStarting with the font helper...\n\nCreating the DaytimeScene enum that maps hours to time-of-day scenes with a static method to determine the current scene based on the hour...\n\nAdding computed properties for gradient colors and clock text color that vary by scene...\n\nNow I'm refactoring HomeView to use the DaytimeScene enum instead of inline color logic, and I need to integrate the TotoroAvatar component into the layout. The spec calls for time, avatar, and alarm list stacked vertically, so I'll position the avatar between the clock header and the alarm list.\n\nFor the AlarmListView, I need to update AlarmCard to use the WatercolorCard wrapper instead of its own background styling. I'm refactoring the card styling to use a custom WatercolorCard component instead of inline modifiers. For the empty state, I need sample alarm data, but since Alarm is a SwiftData model, I'm checking whether I can instantiate it directly without requiring a model context—it looks like the initializer should work fine on its own.\n\nThe tricky