 548 for the alarm list, which is sufficient. I'll now write out all the code files in sequence, starting with the font additions and then moving through each component, and I should also verify the AlarmCard's time display styling.\n\nFor the 40pt semibold rounded font, I'm realizing that `GhibliFonts.clock` uses bold weight while the original uses semibold, so I need to either accept the bold variant or create a specific helper — but since the brief emphasizes using theme tokens without adding too many custom helpers, I'll go with `GhibliFonts.clock(40)` and let the bold weight work as-is.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the TotoroAvatar timer implementation — I switched from `.onAppear` to `.onReceive(blinkTimer)`, but Timer.publish doesn't fire immediately on subscription, so I need to handle that timing carefully.\n\nActually, the behavior should be consistent since the old GCD approach also had a 5-second delay before the first blink. The key difference is that with autoconnect, the timer keeps publishing even when the view isn't visible, but `.onReceive` only delivers events while the view is in the hierarchy, so it effectively pauses when the view disappears — which matches the old behavior. I'll note this in the implementation.\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the timer lifecycle here. Since `blinkTimer` is a stored property without `@State`, it gets recreated with each view render, which could spawn multiple timer instances. But actually, `.onReceive` manages the subscription lifecycle properly, so SwiftUI should handle this correctly without creating resource leaks. I'll stick with `DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter` for the 120ms reopening delay since it's clean and pairs well with `Timer.publish` for the 5-second blink interval. Now I'm ready to write out the full implementation. One thing I need to verify though—the Alarm model doesn't seem to have a `soundFileName` property, but the AlarmScheduler spec references it.  Since I'm not implementing AlarmScheduler yet, 